  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "This electric kettle switches itself off when the water boils. A small steam-activated thermostat makes that automatic decision. While heating, an electric element sends heat into the water. When the water boils, steam rises through a small channel toward the thermostat. Inside it, a bimetallic strip made of two metals bends as steam heats it. The two metals expand by different amounts, forcing the strip to curve. That movement trips a mechanical switch and disconnects the heating element. So boiling water creates the steam signal that physically cuts the kettle's power.",
